 ],
 "literal": "Victorious spear … / lying in the front yard, to the greatly intoxicated man, / whether there is a deer skin or whether there is a mat, / whatever there is, give quickly! / To satisfy the desire … / … to us and to others, / having given to anyone, he accepted sleep.",
 "translation": "With his victorious spear ... To the great warrior lying drunk in the front yard, give quickly whatever is available, whether it be a deer skin or a mat. To satisfy the desire ... having given to us, to others, and to anyone, he has now accepted his final sleep.",
 "modern": "வெற்றி தரும் வேலை உடைய ... முன்றிலில் கிடக்கும் பெரும் கள்ளுண்ட வீரனுக்கு, மான்தோல் இருந்தாலும் பாய் இருந்தாலும், எது இருந்தாலும் விரைவாகக் கொடுங்கள். விருப்பம் தீர ... நமக்கும் பிறருக்கும் யாருக்கும் வாரி வழங்கிவிட்டு அவன் இப்போது துயில்கொள்கிறான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Vāgai tiṇai and Vallāṇ Mullai tuṟai, which praises the strength and generosity of a brave warrior. The poet, Māvempaṟṟūrk Kumaraṉār, describes a generous warrior who lies in his front yard, deeply intoxicated or perhaps fallen in battle. The speaker urges others to quickly bring whatever bedding is available, whether a simple deer skin or a woven mat, to comfort him. The warrior was known for his boundless generosity, having given away everything he owned to poets, to the speaker, and to anyone who asked. Having exhausted all his wealth in giving, he has now accepted his final, peaceful sleep.",
